WEBVTT

1
00:00:12.506 --> 00:00:13.967
Welcome, Little Fox readers.

2
00:00:14.648 --> 00:00:15.389
My name is Mr.

3
00:00:15.449 --> 00:00:21.253
Fay. My students and I just started an exciting new social studies unit: world landmarks.

4
00:00:22.114 --> 00:00:25.497
We're learning about some of the world's most famous places and buildings.

5
00:00:26.337 --> 00:00:30.581
Each week Melissa, Jake, or Alex will tell you about what we discovered.

6
00:00:31.101 --> 00:00:32.222
We're glad that you can join us!

7
00:00:33.063 --> 00:00:34.404
Yesterday was our first lesson.

8
00:00:35.104 --> 00:00:38.587
We studied the Grand Canyon, a huge canyon in the United States.

9
00:00:39.447 --> 00:00:41.108
Melissa is going to tell you all about it.

10
00:00:41.969 --> 00:00:45.431
So sit back and relax as you learn about this amazing place!

11
00:00:46.572 --> 00:00:47.313
Hi everyone!

12
00:00:48.033 --> 00:00:48.533
As Mr.

13
00:00:48.613 --> 00:00:54.558
Fay said, yesterday our class learned about the Grand Canyon, located in the state of Arizona.

14
00:00:55.398 --> 00:00:59.421
At first I thought it was going to be boring, but it wasn't at all!

15
00:01:00.161 --> 00:01:03.624
Mr. Fay began with the story of how the canyon was made.

16
00:01:04.360 --> 00:01:09.922
He said that millions of years ago, a river began flowing over layers of stone.

17
00:01:10.563 --> 00:01:18.206
This river—known today as the Colorado River—made grooves in the sheets of rock that grew deeper and wider.

18
00:01:18.966 --> 00:01:24.108
Over time the rock became a long valley with steep walls: the Grand Canyon.

19
00:01:25.429 --> 00:01:30.231
Some rocks in the canyon are about 2.5 billion years old.

20
00:01:30.891 --> 00:01:37.591
Because the canyon is so old, there are some really interesting things in the rocks, like ancient shells.

21
00:01:38.312 --> 00:01:45.757
You can also find other cool fossils, like ones of sponges, fish teeth, and animal footprints.

22
00:01:46.597 --> 00:01:54.303
In the twentieth century, scientists found something else in the canyon: small twig figures shaped like deer and

23
00:01:54.363 --> 00:01:55.083
other animals.

24
00:01:55.924 --> 00:01:59.546
Scientists think these figures are thousands of years old.

25
00:02:00.207 --> 00:02:07.176
The figures show that humans, especially Native Americans, have been living in the Grand Canyon region for a

26
00:02:07.336 --> 00:02:08.437
very long time.

27
00:02:09.237 --> 00:02:14.481
Europeans and Americans didn't really know much about the canyon until 1869.

28
00:02:15.161 --> 00:02:22.787
That's when an American geologist named John Wesley Powell decided to lead the first official river expedition through

29
00:02:22.827 --> 00:02:23.347
the canyon.

30
00:02:24.288 --> 00:02:29.832
Powell and his men faced many hardships, especially treacherous currents and harsh weather.

31
00:02:30.716 --> 00:02:34.158
It must have been extra hard for Powell because, guess what?

32
00:02:34.778 --> 00:02:35.979
He only had one arm!

33
00:02:36.739 --> 00:02:41.582
As the men explored, they took pictures, studied rocks, and drew maps.

34
00:02:42.162 --> 00:02:44.303
Then they shared the canyon with the world.

35
00:02:45.004 --> 00:02:50.847
Powell even named it, calling it the Grand Canyon because of its size and magnificent beauty.

36
00:02:51.767 --> 00:02:53.709
In 1919 the U.S.

37
00:02:53.729 --> 00:02:59.912
government turned the Grand Canyon into a national park to protect the canyon's wildlife and landscape.

38
00:03:00.892 --> 00:03:07.857
Now almost five million people go to Arizona each year to visit Grand Canyon National Park.

39
00:03:08.918 --> 00:03:12.460
Mr. Fay said the best way to see the canyon is by hiking.

40
00:03:13.140 --> 00:03:19.945
The park has short walks along the canyon's edge, but it also has a 34-kilometer hike that goes

41
00:03:20.025 --> 00:03:22.607
along the bottom of the entire canyon.

42
00:03:22.627 --> 00:03:24.908
34 kilometers!

43
00:03:25.789 --> 00:03:30.748
If you're like me and just the thought of walking that far wears you out, you can see

44
00:03:30.768 --> 00:03:35.710
the canyon in other ways—such as by helicopter, boat, or mule.

45
00:03:35.730 --> 00:03:39.071
(Personally I'd choose the helicopter!)

46
00:03:39.751 --> 00:03:45.553
If you do go hiking down to the bottom, be sure to take plenty of water, sunscreen, and

47
00:03:45.613 --> 00:03:46.013
a map.

48
00:03:46.773 --> 00:03:49.514
Also be prepared for all kinds of weather.

49
00:03:50.314 --> 00:03:55.716
Mr. Fay said the canyon is so deep that the weather changes drastically as you descend.

50
00:03:56.852 --> 00:04:01.834
When he visited in the winter, he was hiking along through some snow near the rim.

51
00:04:02.454 --> 00:04:06.375
But later, when he reached the bottom, it was hot and sunny!

52
00:04:07.075 --> 00:04:08.136
Next Mr.

53
00:04:08.176 --> 00:04:11.597
Fay showed us pictures of animals that live in the canyon today.

54
00:04:12.357 --> 00:04:20.520
We saw photos of bighorn sheep standing on rocky cliffs, beavers chomping on trees, and eagles soaring in

55
00:04:20.560 --> 00:04:21.080
the sky.

56
00:04:21.864 --> 00:04:27.607
Mr. Fay also showed us pictures of scary animals, like coyotes and mountain lions.

57
00:04:28.428 --> 00:04:34.451
He said to watch out for coyotes especially because they'll come right into your campsite looking for food.

58
00:04:35.272 --> 00:04:40.294
Some other scary animals in the canyon are rattlesnakes and scorpions.

59
00:04:41.155 --> 00:04:43.156
I didn't like seeing pictures of them.

60
00:04:43.636 --> 00:04:44.877
They give me the creeps!

61
00:04:46.198 --> 00:04:53.137
One animal we learned a lot about was the California condor, the largest flying bird in North America.

62
00:04:53.858 --> 00:04:57.519
I was really sad when I found out that condors are endangered.

63
00:04:58.460 --> 00:05:02.782
In 1987 there were less than ten left in the whole world!

64
00:05:03.462 --> 00:05:11.206
But thanks to the work of conservationists, California condors are returning to the Grand Canyon, and their numbers

65
00:05:11.266 --> 00:05:11.846
are rising.

66
00:05:12.586 --> 00:05:15.528
Mr. Fay actually got to see one when he visited.

67
00:05:16.244 --> 00:05:22.147
He said that when condors fly, their gigantic, flapping wings make the sound of thunder!

68
00:05:22.167 --> 00:05:25.769
The Grand Canyon is really amazing.

69
00:05:26.490 --> 00:05:31.893
However, like a lot of Earth's beautiful natural landmarks, it is also in danger.

70
00:05:32.673 --> 00:05:39.737
Because of pollution and tourism, the land is being worn down and animal habitats are being destroyed.

71
00:05:40.597 --> 00:05:45.040
Luckily some groups are working to protect the canyon—and you can help too.

72
00:05:45.980 --> 00:05:51.402
If you're ever there, be sure to pick up your trash, stay on the marked paths, leave the

73
00:05:51.462 --> 00:05:55.003
wild animals alone, and tell others to do the same.

74
00:05:56.283 --> 00:06:01.565
After we finished learning about the Grand Canyon, our class made a big mural of it.

75
00:06:02.205 --> 00:06:08.167
My friends painted a condor flying over the canyon and a one-armed man in a boat on the

76
00:06:08.207 --> 00:06:12.108
river. I added myself, waving from a helicopter.

77
00:06:12.936 --> 00:06:15.797
I think our teacher described the Grand Canyon best.

78
00:06:16.597 --> 00:06:20.078
He said it's like "a book that tells the history of the earth."

79
00:06:20.759 --> 00:06:26.240
I know what he meant now: the layers of the canyon's ancient walls hold the story of life

80
00:06:26.320 --> 00:06:27.101
on our planet.

81
00:06:27.841 --> 00:06:29.281
What an awesome place!

82
00:06:30.122 --> 00:06:31.482
That's all for now, everyone.

83
00:06:32.062 --> 00:06:34.623
I wonder what our next landmarks lesson will be about.

84
00:06:35.203 --> 00:06:37.184
I guess we'll find out together next week!
